he problem states \"Validate every required field before computing a result.\" So we should validate and raise if invalid. Since the evaluator likely provides valid cases, but we must be robust. We'll include validation.\n\nNow write the code string. Use triple quotes? But we need JSON string, so need to escape double quotes inside. We'll use single quotes for strings inside Python? But inside JSON we must escape double quotes. Simpler: Write Python code with double quotes for strings (like \"word_token_indices\") and then in JSON, escape each double quote as \\\". For newlines, use \\n. For backslashes, use \\\\.\n\nWe'll write the code as a normal string with indentation. Then use json.dumps to escape, but since we are generating JSON manually, we must do it ourselves. We'll manually escape.\n\nAlternatively, write the code as a multi-line string and then use Python to produce the JSON, but we are not executing Python now. Since we are the assistant, we can produce the JSON manually.\n\nI'll write the code without any complex quotes; use consistent single quotes for dictionary keys? But Python allows single quotes. That might be easier to include in JSON as it doesn't require escaping double quotes. However JSON requires double quotes for string values, but the Python code itself can use single quotes. That's fine: the Python source string will contain single quotes, which are allowed in JSON string (no need to escape). So we can write Python code with single-quoted strings to avoid escaping multiple times.\n\nThus, code:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    word_token_indices = case['word_token_indices']\n    word_labels = case['word_labels']\n    token_count = case['token_count']\n    # Validation\n    if not (len(word_token_indices) == len(word_labels)):\n        raise ValueError('Mismatched lengths')\n    # Check out-of-range, duplicates, uncovered\n    seen = set()\n    total = 0\n    for group in word_token_indices:\n        for idx in group:\n            if not (0 <= idx < token_count):\n                raise ValueError(f'Index {idx} out of range')\n            if idx in seen:\n                raise ValueError(f'Duplicate index {idx}')\n            seen.add(idx)\n            total += 1\n    if total != token_count:\n        raise ValueError(f'Uncovered tokens: {token_count - total} missing')\n    token_labels = [0] * token_count\n    for group, label in zip(word_token_indices, word_labels):\n        for idx in group:\n            token_labels[idx] = label\n    kept_indices = [i for i, v in enumerate(token_labels) if v == 1]\n    return {'kept_indices': kept_indices, 'token_labels': token_labels}\n\nThat should work.\n\nNow produce JSON output.\n\nWe'll create a Python dict with key \"implementation\" and value the above code as a string. Then convert to JSON manually.\n\nWe'll need to escape newlines: replace newline with \\n. Also escape backslashes: none here. Also escape double quotes if any: we used single quotes, so none. Also escape forward slashes? Not needed.\n\nWe'll write the string with explicit \\n for each line break. To avoid mistakes, we can write the code in a single line? But easier to write with \\n. Let's do:\n\n\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    word_token_indices = case['word_token_indices']\\n    word_labels = case['word_labels']\\n    token_count = case['token_count']\\n    # Validation\\n    if not (len(word_token_indices) == len(word_labels)):\\n        raise ValueError('Mismatched lengths')\\n    # Check out-of-range, duplicates, uncovered\\n    seen = set()\\n    total = 0\\n    for group in word_token_indices:\\n        for idx in group:\\n            if not (0 <= idx < token_count):\\n                raise ValueError(f'Index {idx} out of range')\\n            if idx in seen:\\n                raise ValueError(f'Duplicate index {idx}')\\n            seen.add(idx)\\n            total += 1\\n    if total != token_count:\\n        raise ValueError(f'Uncovered tokens: {token_count - total} missin